{{ ref: 37db2572-f788-81ae-92d1-ee71fe083d4a }}
## 🤔 Problem Statement
--{ ref: 37db2572-f788-81ae-92d1-ee71fe083d4a }--

{{ ref: 37db2572-f788-81df-87db-c13870eff591 }}
Large codebases — including our own `theriety/core` monorepo — accumulate entanglement faster than humans can audit it: import cycles between files, barrels that obscure true dependencies, 1,000-line files nobody dares touch, symbols exported "just in case", and test helpers that quietly leak into production paths. Reviews catch local violations; nobody can see the global structure.
--{ ref: 37db2572-f788-81df-87db-c13870eff591 }--

{{ ref: 30ef099b-6a41-4408-bd70-ffe371302a40 }}
The mathematical problem underneath is **hierarchical acyclic module decomposition**: given a directed multigraph of symbols and types (with typed, weighted edges from value imports, type references, inheritance, and calls), find a laminar container tree — symbols → files → folders → domains → packages → package groups — such that:
--{ ref: 30ef099b-6a41-4408-bd70-ffe371302a40 }--

{{ ref: 4a1b2152-ef7d-4d96-8c30-10e4e6c1e1ab }}
1. at **every** level, sibling groups form a DAG over hard edges (one-way flow everywhere)
--{ ref: 4a1b2152-ef7d-4d96-8c30-10e4e6c1e1ab }--
{{ ref: 821b5eb4-d8eb-4b4b-87c5-995411e8bd0c }}
1. production never depends on test code (three-valued polarity: Production | TestCase | TestSupport)
--{ ref: 821b5eb4-d8eb-4b4b-87c5-995411e8bd0c }--
{{ ref: 5ed69388-968d-4876-903e-fbebf152b8a6 }}
1. every container respects its capacity (250 production SLOC per file by default; member caps above)
--{ ref: 5ed69388-968d-4876-903e-fbebf152b8a6 }--
{{ ref: 310c5d54-1f8f-493e-aab2-21b4f0b08c15 }}
1. cut edges are minimized, weighted by how high in the tree they cross (cheap within a file, expensive across packages)
--{ ref: 310c5d54-1f8f-493e-aab2-21b4f0b08c15 }--
{{ ref: 955ddcc8-ea83-421d-8d8d-032799944667 }}
1. results are **diverse**: up to k genuinely different candidates per mode, because architecture is taste as well as math
--{ ref: 955ddcc8-ea83-421d-8d8d-032799944667 }--

{{ ref: 695521d1-ce38-4791-bb6b-c5b5bc3d34e6 }}
Key subproblems are classical and hard: minimum feedback arc set (NP-hard) for cycle-break suggestions, acyclic graph partitioning (NP-hard) for clustering, capacitated packing for file assignment. Strata composes exact solvers where bounded (ILP on SCCs ≤ 300 nodes) with principled heuristics elsewhere, and is honest about which is which.
--{ ref: 695521d1-ce38-4791-bb6b-c5b5bc3d34e6 }--

{{ ref: 3f02eed1-8794-4b25-92ff-b14368cd8d1e }}
Strata is a **diagnostic** tool: it reports violations of the current structure and proposes restructures with full delta narration — the developer decides; the tool never rewrites code.
--{ ref: 3f02eed1-8794-4b25-92ff-b14368cd8d1e }--

